cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Acid Mine Drainage (AMD) and Coal Mine Drainage (CMD) Detection System: a validated reimplementation of USGS SIM 3466, with its measured limits"
version: 3.11.0
date-released: 2026-09-19
# Concept DOI: resolves to the newest Zenodo version. 10.5281/zenodo.19429983 is
# version 1.5.5 (2026-04-05) only, whose description carries retracted claims.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.19424985
authors:
  - family-names: "Hussein"
    given-names: "Abdulrahman"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-0401-9219"
    affiliation: "Kent State University, Department of Earth Sciences, Environmental Remote Sensing Laboratory"
repository-code: "https://github.com/abdulrahman-R-A-hussein/AMD-Detection-Tool"
url: "https://www.climtawy.com"
abstract: >
  An open reimplementation of the automated iron-sulfate mineral mapping method published by Rockwell
  and Gnesda (2021, USGS SIM 3466), which released a method and a result raster but no code, together
  with a pre-registered programme measuring what that method can and cannot do. The land arm reproduces
  all six published index formulas exactly; three departures this project had introduced as improvements
  were measured as regressions, and correcting them raised worst-case leave-one-site-out Youden J from
  0.107 to 0.440 against the published map. Agreement with that map is replica fidelity, not accuracy:
  only measured field chemistry is treated as ground truth.
  Against 86 chemically confirmed mine-discharge points across four Colorado districts, scored on
  USGS and EPA water chemistry, detection is a measured NULL - all nine candidate indices fail all
  three control tiers, the best case reaching Youden J 0.234 against a pre-registered bar of 0.25.
  What does survive is severity ranking: a red-to-blue band ratio tracks measured dissolved iron at
  Spearman rho +0.568 (n=75, within-region permutation p=0.0004), positive in three of four
  districts (Leadville rho +0.004, n=23), but with negative leave-one-region-out R2 for every index-analyte pair - so it ranks
  severity within a district and does not predict concentration across districts. Detection and
  severity are both flat from 10 m to 100 m pixel size, so spatial resolution is not the binding
  constraint in that range. In Appalachian coal watersheds a vegetation index tracks measured sulfate
  and conductance negatively and sign-consistently, at a scale that differs between basins.
  A pre-registered blind search (2026-09-15) found no evidence that the index finds mine sources
  it was not told about: no signal in districts never used to choose it, and no advantage over
  bare ground where it was chosen.
  IMPORTANT - RETRACTIONS. Versions up to and including 1.5.5 (the last version archived on
  Zenodo before this one) claimed a validated in-water
  contamination-detection capability, including validation against Muskingum Watershed (Ohio)
  chemistry and detection of high-sulfate water at Ganau Pond, Iraq. Those claims are RETRACTED.
  The Ohio water column is a measured null; the Ganau result was circular, because the thresholds
  were tuned until that site scored as contaminated; and the water mask that produced the apparently
  clean controls admitted zero water pixels at two of them. Sulfate has no VNIR absorption and no
  optical sulfate detection is claimed at any concentration - any apparent signal is iron, turbidity,
  or colour that co-varies with sulfate. The full record, including every retraction and the
  pre-registrations that were committed before their data, is in the repository under validation/.
  Earlier work presented at the 40th Annual Graduate Research Symposium, Kent State University.
